(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-5-cardiomyopathy-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Cardiomyopathy Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 5","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 5","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which history precedes viral myocarditis?","choices":["A flulike illness 1 to 2 weeks before","Chest trauma 1 to 2 days before","Years of heavy alcohol intake","An emotional shock hours before"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Viral myocarditis follows a recent (1 to 2 week) flulike syndrome with fever, myalgia, fatigue, respiratory symptoms or gastroenteritis before the heart failure symptoms. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Why are nitrates avoided in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["They cause complete heart block","They raise the blood pressure","They thicken the septum further","They worsen outflow obstruction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that reduce preload or afterload, such as nitrates, may increase symptoms by aggravating outflow tract obstruction, so they are avoided if possible. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Why does squatting soften the murmur of obstructive hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["It slows the heart to a stop","It closes the mitral valve early","More filling reduces obstruction","Less filling increases obstruction"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anything that increases blood flow in the heart reduces the murmur by reducing outflow obstruction; squatting and passive leg raising increase preload. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which condition must be differentiated from restrictive cardiomyopathy because the presentations are similar?","choices":["Takotsubo syndrome","Constrictive pericarditis","Aortic dissection","Viral pneumonia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Constrictive pericarditis must be differentiated because its presenting symptoms are similar; acute pericarditis, tamponade, valvular disease and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy complete the list. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How is the diagnosis of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy confirmed?","choices":["Electrocardiogram changes","Catheterization, normal coronaries","Endomyocardial biopsy results","Troponin level above normal"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is confirmed by cardiac catheterization, which most often shows normal coronary arteries despite the wall-motion abnormality and elevated troponin. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 103"},{"q":"Adverse ventricular remodeling in dilated cardiomyopathy gives the heart which shape?","choices":["Narrow and tubular","Normal-sized, big atria","Thick septal bulge","Globular, or round"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Adverse ventricular remodeling leads to a globular (globe or round) shaped heart, with an increase in total cardiac mass and dilated chambers. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What is the mainstay of treatment for acute myocarditis?","choices":["Supportive care","Septal ablation","Early transplant","Long antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment is directed at the clinical scenario and supportive care is the mainstay, with aggressive hemodynamic stabilization when needed and drugs as required. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"What is the etiology of Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Plaque rupture","Viral infection","Unknown","Amyloid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The etiology of stress cardiomyopathy is unknown; what is known is that a significant emotional or physical stressor precedes it. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"Which laboratory marker indicates cardiac myonecrosis in acute myocarditis?","choices":["Serum amylase","Serum troponin","Serum lipase","Serum ferritin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Serum troponin is the indicator of cardiac myonecrosis; the work-up also includes a blood count for leukocytosis and an erythrocyte sedimentation rate, which may be elevated. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the gold standard for diagnosing acute myocarditis?","choices":["Serum troponin level","Electrocardiogram","Endomyocardial biopsy","Cardiac catheterization"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Endomyocardial biopsy is the gold standard, used to establish the diagnosis once other causes of heart failure have been excluded. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"Which storage disorder deposits iron in heart tissue and causes restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Sarcoidosis","Wilson disease","Hemochromatosis","Amyloidosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hemochromatosis is the storage disorder that deposits iron in heart tissue, one of the causes of restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"Where are patients with Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy admitted?","choices":["Home, no admission","To general surgery","To cardiology","To psychiatry"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because the syndrome mimics acute coronary syndrome with no reliable early differences, patients follow acute coronary syndrome protocols and are admitted to cardiology. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which drug pair should every patient with dilated cardiomyopathy receive, whatever the cause?","choices":["Beta blocker and angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor","Calcium channel blocker and long-acting nitrate agent","Anticoagulant and antiplatelet agent for every patient","Thiazide diuretic and oral potassium supplementation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of dilated cardiomyopathy is essentially that of chronic heart failure, and all patients, regardless of etiology, should get a beta blocker and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"What is the leading cause of mortality in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Endocarditis","Sudden cardiac death","Aortic dissection","Embolic stroke"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The clinical course varies and heart failure symptoms gradually progress, but sudden cardiac death is the leading cause of mortality in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 82"},{"q":"Why are both atria enlarged in restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Leaking mitral leaflets","Septal outflow blockage","Fluid around the heart","Impaired ventricular filling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Stiff ventricular walls impair diastolic filling, so both atria enlarge, and atrial fibrillation is common. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 85"},{"q":"When is an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator indicated in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Any reduced ejection fraction at diagnosis","After a first episode of atrial fibrillation","When the pulse pressure becomes narrow","Ejection fraction below 35% on maximal therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An implantable cardioverter-defibrillator is placed if the ejection fraction is below 35% after maximum medical therapy; a biventricular device combines it with pacing. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"Where do prominent Q waves appear on the electrocardiogram in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["No lead shows Q waves","Inferior and lateral leads","Right precordial leads only","Anterior leads V1 to V3 only"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Prominent Q waves appear in the inferior (II, III, aVF) and lateral (I, aVL, V4 to V6) leads, reflecting septal depolarization of hypertrophied tissue, with left axis deviation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is systolic anterior motion of the mitral valve?","choices":["Valve ring stretching as the ventricle dilates","Leaflets thickened by old rheumatic scarring","Anterior leaflet pulled into the outflow tract","Posterior leaflet prolapsing into the atrium"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. High-velocity turbulent flow pulls the anterior mitral leaflet into the flow tract, causing mid-systolic obstruction of the left ventricular outflow tract and mitral regurgitation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"What is the first principle of managing restrictive cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Surgical septal myectomy","High-dose diuresis first","Immediate transplantation","Treat the underlying disorder"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Management begins by treating the underlying disorder, then diuretics with caution for congestion, rate-slowing drugs, anticoagulation and pacing as needed. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 98"},{"q":"What produces the third heart sound in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Mitral leaflet striking the septum","Blood striking a compliant ventricle","Atrium contracting on a stiff ventricle","Closure of a thickened aortic valve"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The third heart sound gallop comes from a large amount of blood striking a very compliant left ventricle, a sign of volume overload. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"Which description fits the ventricles in dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Stiff, with near-normal cavity size","Ballooned apex that later recovers","Dilated, with impaired contraction","Thick-walled, with a small cavity"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dilated cardiomyopathy is characterized by dilation and impaired contraction of one or both ventricles, which leads to systolic dysfunction. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What does gadolinium add to magnetic resonance imaging in suspected myocarditis?","choices":["Rules out coronary blockage","Measures troponin release","Grows the causative virus","Shows the extent of inflammation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gadolinium-enhanced magnetic resonance imaging improves visibility and shows the extent of myocardial inflammation. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the risk factor for Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["A major emotional or physical stressor","Many traditional cardiac risk factors","A flulike illness 1 to 2 weeks before","Inherited sarcomere gene mutation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The risk factor is a significant emotional or physical stressor: death of a loved one, bad financial news, natural disaster, new illness, surgery, drug withdrawal or near drowning. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 101"},{"q":"What happens to ejection fraction in late-stage hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["It rises above 75%","It falls below 25%","It swings every beat","It stays at exactly 60%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The ejection fraction is usually normal, and in late stages it is elevated above 75% because the small ventricle nearly empties to maintain output. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which laboratory test evaluates for anemia in suspected dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Complete blood count","Liver function panel","Thyroid function tests","Metabolic panel"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The initial laboratory work includes a complete blood count for anemia, a metabolic panel for electrolytes and renal function, liver function and thyroid function. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"Which inheritance pattern is most common in familial dilated cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Mitochondrial (maternal)","Autosomal dominant","Autosomal recessive","X-linked recessive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Familial dilated cardiomyopathy accounts for up to 35% of cases, and autosomal dominant inheritance is the most common pattern. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"When is anticoagulation given in Takotsubo (stress) cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Never, because recovery is usual","If a ventricular thrombus is present","For every patient at diagnosis","Only when coronaries are blocked"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Supportive treatment depends on presentation: anticoagulation if a left ventricular thrombus is present, diuretics if heart failure is present, and beta blockers. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"Which condition is in the differential diagnosis of hypertrophic cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Athletic heart","Aortic dissection","Takotsubo syndrome","Pulmonary embolism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The differential is aortic stenosis, mitral regurgitation, left ventricular hypertrophy from hypertensive heart disease, athletic heart and restrictive cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"When is hospice referral appropriate in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["End-stage cardiomyopathy","At adolescent diagnosis","After a first arrhythmia","Once sodium is restricted"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Palliative care covers symptom management, advance care planning, goals-of-care discussions and caregiver support, with hospice referral in end-stage cardiomyopathy. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"What is the aim of cardiac rehabilitation programs in cardiomyopathy?","choices":["Reversal of thickened ventricular walls","Replacement of all needed medications","Screening of relatives for gene defects","Better exercise tolerance and quality of life"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiac rehabilitation programs aim to improve exercise tolerance and quality of life, with education on medications, nutrition and symptom monitoring and psychological support. — Cardiomyopathy - Carter.pptx, Slide 107"}]};
